# Two things stood in every turn that no turn can change or that nobody can tell apart.
# Whether the view is longer than the mask: both sides are made in front of the loop. In front of the turns laid out it is asked once; who fails takes the loop as written.
# And the hole of `w[i & 7]`: in a product undefined and NaN give the same, so the read may give NaN for its hole where a number or nothing stands beside it. The second key is written behind the first read, so the
# first read did not see what the second gives, kept its hole apart, and the product asked it again. A key that is a number cut by a written mask is an index whoever reads it first.
# One call of eight turns: 263 > 227 instructions; a turn 16 > 11.
# f1 = go
# RUN: compile RelationsOfTurnsAskedOnceInFrontOfTurnsLaidOut mechanisms=all
# CHECK: statistic RelationsOfTurnsAskedOnceInFrontOfTurnsLaidOut 12
# RUN(WALK): walk f1:Body mechanisms=all
# WALK: number @Double = GetArrayElement {{*}}readsHoleAsNaN
# WALK-NEXT: number @Double = GetArrayElement {{*}}readsHoleAsNaN
# WALK-NEXT: = Mul
# RUN(ANSWERS): run-in-shell mechanisms=all
# ANSWERS: 262 NaN NaN 185.5 500 NaN 242 0
# ANSWERS-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(NONE): run-in-shell mechanisms=none
# NONE: 262 NaN NaN 185.5 500 NaN 242 0
# NONE-NEXT: exited with 0
